{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when he sins and is guilty.</b> The duplication of language teaches that he sins both against the Holy One, blessed be He, and against the one he wronged. It teaches you here that one who has robbery in his possession and thereby sins against his fellow also sins against the Holy One, blessed be He. This sin in the parashah is intentional, and therefore it begins 'when a soul sins' (נפש כי תחטא), and did not say 'when she sins unwittingly' (כי תחטא בשגגה); whereas in all the other sins he mentioned 'unwittingly' in each and every parashah. And it is known that all the offerings come only for unwitting sins, but we find among them only four for which one brings an offering for an intentional sin, and they are: one who lies with a slave-girl, the nazirite who became defiled, the oath of testimony, and the oath of a deposit." "<b>And he shall restore the robbery which he robbed.</b> It would have been sufficient for Scripture to say והשיב את הגזלה ('and he shall restore the robbery') — why did it need אשר גזל ('which he robbed')? So that you should not say 'and he shall restore the robbery' meaning the monetary value of the robbery; therefore it needed אשר גזל, to say the very thing itself that he robbed: as long as the robbed object still exists, he is obligated to restore it in kind if it is still in his possession in kind. And if he has already mixed it with permitted property, the law in that case is to pay the monetary value of the robbed object. This is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said in Bava Kamma: if one robbed a beam and built it into a building, Beit Shammai say: dismantle the entire building and return the beam to its owner; and Beit Hillel say: he has only the monetary value of the beam alone, on account of the ordinance to facilitate the penitent's return; and the halakhah follows Beit Hillel. And the repentance of the people of Nineveh, about which Scripture testified concerning them (Jonah 3:8): 'ומן החמס אשר בכפיהם' — and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said of them: one who robbed a beam and built it into a building should dismantle the entire building and return the beam to its owner — beyond the strict line of the law they acted. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded <b>אשר גזל</b> — 'in the form that he robbed,' to tell you that if it depreciated afterward, he is obligated to pay according to its value at the time of the robbery. And the same law applies also to oppression (עושק) and to a deposit (פקדון), and therefore Scripture mentioned concerning them אשר עשק — 'in the form that he oppressed,' אשר הפקד אתו — 'in the form that was deposited with him.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:20.143226684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}